@page "/docs/tailwind"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>Tailwind · blux-ui</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Tailwind</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">Compile your own Tailwind CSS, with the Blux theme, when you run <code>dotnet build</code>. No Node, no npm: the build downloads the official Tailwind standalone CLI once and runs it.</p>

    <h2>Turn it on</h2>
    <p>Set one property in the project that owns the stylesheet, and write the stylesheet. It is the usual Tailwind v4 entry: it imports Tailwind and the theme that every build copies to <code>obj/blux/blux.theme.css</code> (see <a href="/docs/installation">Installation</a>).</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Project" />
    <CodeBlock Title="Styles/app.css" Code="@Css" />
    <p>The theme also lists the classes the Blux components use, so they are generated with yours: there is nothing to add for them. The CLI finds your <code>.razor</code>, <code>.cs</code> and <code>.html</code> files itself, starting from the project folder; <code>obj</code> and <code>bin</code> are left out. Use <code>@@source</code> in the stylesheet for any other folder (a shared project, say), and <code>@@import "tailwindcss" source(none)</code> if you want to list every source yourself.</p>

    <h2>Where the CSS goes</h2>
    <p>The result is not written into your source tree. It is generated into <code>obj/&lt;configuration&gt;/&lt;framework&gt;/blux/tailwind/</code> and added to the static web assets, so it is served like any file of <code>wwwroot</code> and fingerprinted by <code>@@Assets[...]</code>. The route is <code>app.css</code> in a web project and <code>_content/&lt;Project&gt;/app.css</code> in a Razor class library, which is what a MAUI Blazor Hybrid host or a web host that references the library links to. That is why it is generated into <code>obj</code> and not <code>wwwroot</code>: the same setting works in both kinds of project, and a library does not need a generated file checked in.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="App.razor" Code="@Link" />
    <p>In Release the CSS is minified. <code>dotnet watch</code> rebuilds when the stylesheet changes: it is declared as a watched file.</p>

    <h2>Sister packages</h2>
<p>
    <code>Blux.UI.Tools</code> and the packages after it have no precompiled stylesheet, so they need Tailwind compiled by your app. With <code>BluxTailwind=true</code> nothing else is needed: when the package is
    referenced, the generated entry also imports <code>obj/blux/blux-tools.theme.css</code> (the classes of the package's components), so the single compiled sheet holds Blux.UI, Blux.UI.Tools and your own classes,
    variants included. With your own Tailwind pipeline, add the import yourself and set <code>&lt;BluxToolsOwnTailwind&gt;true&lt;/BluxToolsOwnTailwind&gt;</code> in the project, which tells the build that you compile Tailwind;
    without either, the build warns with <code>BLUXTOOLS001</code>.
</p>

<h2>The binary</h2>
    <p>The build downloads <code>tailwindcss-&lt;os&gt;-&lt;arch&gt;</code> for the machine that builds from the GitHub release of the pinned version (4.3.5, the same one the library is built with) and keeps it in a cache folder. It is checked against a SHA-256 that ships inside the package and discarded if it differs, and the executable bit is set on Linux and macOS. The hash is computed once per cached binary: a stamp next to it records its size and modification time, later builds trust the stamp, and a file that changed since is hashed again and deleted when it differs, so an unverified binary is never run. Linux x64 and arm64 (glibc and musl), macOS x64 and arm64, and Windows x64 are supported; Windows on ARM runs the x64 binary. The release has no other builds.</p>

    <h2>Properties</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><code>BluxTailwind</code>: <code>true</code> turns the feature on. Default <code>false</code>.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindInput</code>: the stylesheet, relative to the project. Default <code>Styles/app.css</code>.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindAssetPath</code>: where it is served, below the project's base path. Default <code>app.css</code>.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindMinify</code>: default <code>true</code> in Release.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindExecutable</code>: a Tailwind binary to use instead of the download, with no check. A path that does not exist is an error.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindVersion</code>: the release to download. Only the default has embedded checksums; another version is refused, so use <code>BluxTailwindExecutable</code> for it.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindCacheDir</code>: the cache of binaries. Default: one folder per user, shared by every project and solution (<code>~/.blux/tailwind</code> on Linux and macOS, <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Blux\tailwind</code> on Windows), so the binary is downloaded once per machine. Like the NuGet cache it is never pruned and can be deleted at any time: the next build downloads and verifies the binary again. The stamp is trusted as far as the folder is: someone who can rewrite the file while keeping its size and modification time can also replace the stamp. Keep it between CI runs to download once there too.</li>
        <li><code>BluxTailwindAdditionalInput</code>: files outside the project that the stylesheet reads (items, globs allowed). See below.</li>
        <li><code>BluxOffline</code>: <code>true</code> never touches the network.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Shared projects</h2>
    <p>The CLI reads what <code>@@source</code> points at, but MSBuild only knows the files of the project folder (and the stylesheet itself, even outside it). For a folder outside the project, add an <code>@@source</code> to the stylesheet and list the same files as additional inputs so that editing them rebuilds the CSS:</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Shared" />
    <CodeBlock Title="Styles/app.css" Code="@SharedCss" />

    <h2>Offline builds</h2>
    <p>The download is the only network use. Fill the cache with one online build and set <code>BluxOffline=true</code> afterwards, or install Tailwind another way and give its path to <code>BluxTailwindExecutable</code>. When the download is impossible, the build fails with <code>BLUXTW001</code>, an error that names <code>BluxTailwindExecutable</code>, because without the CSS the app would run unstyled.</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Site.csproj" Code="@Offline" />

    <h2>Incremental builds</h2>
    <p>The CLI runs again only when a <code>.razor</code>, <code>.cs</code>, <code>.cshtml</code>, <code>.html</code> or <code>.css</code> file of the project, the Blux theme or the binary changed. The output lives in <code>obj</code>, which is not an input, so a watch loop does not trigger itself. Design-time builds (an IDE refreshing IntelliSense) and <code>dotnet publish --no-build</code> never run it (the stylesheet is already part of what the build produced).</p>

    <h2>dotnet watch</h2>
    <p>Under <code>dotnet watch</code> (checked on SDK 10.0.404) an edit to the stylesheet or a <code>.razor</code> file makes the build targets evaluate again and the CSS is regenerated, but not reliably in time, and the browser is not told: watch does not follow files under <code>obj</code>, and it does not refresh the page for a CSS-only edit. The reliable way is a second terminal, next to <code>dotnet watch</code>, that runs the CLI with its own file watcher:</p>
    <CodeBlock Title="Second terminal" Code="@Watch" />
    <p>It finds and verifies the binary like the build does and rewrites the same file in <code>obj</code> on every edit of a <code>.razor</code>, <code>.cs</code>, <code>.html</code> or <code>.css</code> file. Reload the page by hand to see the new CSS. Ctrl+C stops it and the CLI with it. Build the project once before, so the theme is copied and the stylesheet is part of the static web assets. The project templates will ship a VS Code task that starts it.</p>
</div>

@code {
    private const string Project = """
        <PropertyGroup>
          <BluxTailwind>true</BluxTailwind>
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;

    private const string Css = """
        @import "tailwindcss";
        @import "../obj/blux/blux.theme.css";

        /* your own CSS, and @source for folders the scan does not reach */
        """;

    private const string Link = """
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
        """;

    private const string Watch = """
        dotnet build -t:BluxTailwindWatch
        """;

    private const string Shared = """
        <ItemGroup>
          <BluxTailwindAdditionalInput Include="../Shared/**/*.razor;../Shared/**/*.cs" />
        </ItemGroup>
        """;

    private const string SharedCss = """
        @import "tailwindcss";
        @import "../obj/blux/blux.theme.css";
        @source "../../Shared";
        """;

    private const string Offline = """
        <PropertyGroup>
          <BluxTailwind>true</BluxTailwind>
          <BluxTailwindExecutable>/usr/local/bin/tailwindcss</BluxTailwindExecutable>
          <!-- or: BluxOffline=true with a cache filled by an earlier build -->
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;
}
